        <div id="post-859" class="post-859 post type-post status-publish format-standard hentry category-craftsmanship">

          <div class="post-header">
           <h3 class="post-title"><a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2011/01/programming-is-not-a-craft-but-you-can-be-a-craftsman/" rel="bookmark" title="Permanent Link: Programming is not a craft… but you can be a craftsman">Programming is not a craft… but you can be a craftsman</a></h3>
           <p class="post-date">
            <span class="month">Jan</span>
            <span class="day">14</span>
           </p>
           <p class="post-author">
            <span class="info">Posted by <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/author/cromwellryan/" title="Posts by cromwellryan ">cromwellryan</a> in <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/category/craftsmanship/" title="View all posts in Craftsmanship" rel="category tag">Craftsmanship</a> | <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2011/01/programming-is-not-a-craft-but-you-can-be-a-craftsman/#respond" class="comments"  title="Comment on Programming is not a craft… but you can be a craftsman"><span class="dsq-postid" rel="859 http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2011/01/programming-is-not-a-craft-but-you-can-be-a-craftsman/">No Comments</span></a>              </span>
           </p>
          </div>

          <div class="post-content clearfix">
          <p>Craftmanship was the software development phrase d’jour in 2010 (or maybe REST, it was a close race).&#160; To start 2011 Dan North very abruptly stated that Programming is not a craft.&#160; I happened to read this a few days before Codemash which was keynoted by Chad Fowler where he very conveniently discussed the Passionate Programmer.&#160; [...]</p>

                    </div>
        </div>
